WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.502
MAN 1: This is the
Armed Forces Tournament.

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:04.137
Tonight, the dueling begins.

00:00:04.204 --> 00:00:07.540
Up first, the two branch duel,
where a veteran from the Army

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.744
and the Air Force goes up
against a veteran from the Navy

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:12.111
and the Air Force.

00:00:12.212 --> 00:00:15.482
Next to me we have a huge
weapons crate with four

00:00:15.582 --> 00:00:17.216
handles coming out of it.

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:19.118
Now, these are
attached to blades

00:00:19.184 --> 00:00:21.554
from iconic military history.

00:00:21.621 --> 00:00:23.189
There's only four
blades here, because you

00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:25.491
are the second duel to take
part in this tournament.

00:00:25.591 --> 00:00:27.360
What I want you guys
to do is come together,

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.095
talk amongst
yourselves and decide

00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:31.631
which one of these blades you
want to build based solely

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:32.965
on the handle you can see.

00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:34.033
 I'm thinking five.

00:00:34.099 --> 00:00:35.201
 I'm good with it.

00:00:35.268 --> 00:00:35.735
Do five.

00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:36.536
 All right.

00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:37.703
Rock on, chief.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:38.938
 All right, gentlemen.

00:00:39.005 --> 00:00:39.838
You guys are going
to be building this.

00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:43.309
[music playing]

00:00:43.876 --> 00:00:46.078
 Yeah.

00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:49.782
 This is the World War
II Air Force survival axe.

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:51.483
DAVE (VOICEOVER): The
United States Army Air Force

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.120
survival axe was a staple
in US bomber planes

00:00:54.187 --> 00:00:55.688
during World War II.

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:59.525
The standard issue tool featured
an axe head and sharp pike that

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:02.161
could be used to stab and
slice through aircraft aluminum

00:01:02.227 --> 00:01:05.531
skin after a crash,
while its rubber handle

00:01:05.631 --> 00:01:08.634
provided protection
from electrical shock.

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:10.669
Although this axe
isn't used much today,

00:01:10.736 --> 00:01:15.007
its legacy lives on in the
2014 video game "The Forest."

00:01:15.074 --> 00:01:15.975
MAN 1: All right, gentlemen.

00:01:16.042 --> 00:01:16.876
Good luck.

00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:18.010
Your time starts now.

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:19.378
JOE STICKEL: My
name's Joe Stickel.

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:21.681 line:8%
I'm a retired Chief Master
Sergeant for the Oregon

00:01:21.781 --> 00:01:23.015 line:14%
International Guard.

00:01:23.115 --> 00:01:25.184
I went in the army right
out of high school.

00:01:25.285 --> 00:01:28.555
And then I served Active
Guard with Kingsley Field

00:01:28.655 --> 00:01:30.090
in Klamath Falls, Oregon.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:33.058
So what I decided I'm going
to do is make two billets.

00:01:33.125 --> 00:01:34.860
I get the first
billet in the forge,

00:01:34.961 --> 00:01:36.495
I figure I can get
that heating up

00:01:36.562 --> 00:01:39.065
and then I start cleaning
my second billet.

00:01:39.131 --> 00:01:41.067
It's coming along really nice.

00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:43.235
It looks like it's good weld.

00:01:43.335 --> 00:01:46.605
So now I've got to twist it.

00:01:46.672 --> 00:01:48.842
MAN 2: Joe is twisting
his billet right now,

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:49.808
but using the press.

00:01:49.875 --> 00:01:50.910
- The press.
- Yeah.

00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:52.044
 It's awesome.

00:01:52.111 --> 00:01:53.613
JOE STICKEL: I cut
the excess off,

00:01:53.679 --> 00:01:58.584
I need to draw some width to
it to get the axe shape to it.

00:01:58.651 --> 00:02:02.722
And then I need to pull out
my spike on the other side.

00:02:02.788 --> 00:02:03.489
DAVE: There we go.

00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:04.690
Look.

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:06.492
We've got some shape
happening over here on Joe.

00:02:06.559 --> 00:02:08.293
JOE STICKEL: I
drifted in the hole,

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:11.330
I realized that that
cutting edge, width-wise,

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:12.899
is entirely too thin.

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:15.601
And that's what they're going
to be hitting that thing into.

00:02:15.701 --> 00:02:18.371
I had plenty of Damascus
already done up.

00:02:18.437 --> 00:02:20.239
So what I'm going to
do is take this piece,

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:23.509
make it into a C-shape,
make sure I got long enough

00:02:23.575 --> 00:02:27.046
for each side, match it up to
the cutting edge of the axe

00:02:27.146 --> 00:02:28.648
and then I'll forge weld it.

00:02:28.714 --> 00:02:31.451
I'm feeling pressured, but I
know I'm going to get it done.

00:02:31.517 --> 00:02:34.186
MAN 1: Joe's quenching, guys.

00:02:34.286 --> 00:02:35.688
JOE STICKEL: My welds
appeared to hold.

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:39.092
I think it came out about
as good as can be expected.

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:41.094
I have to turn in
a finished product.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:42.662
It's tough, but there's
no quitting now.

00:02:42.728 --> 00:02:44.830
I mean, I came here
to do a job and that's

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:46.265
what I'm going to do.

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:48.734
So I just go ahead and
go with the quarter wrap.

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:51.336
I'm happy with the fact
that I got something done.

00:02:51.436 --> 00:02:53.606
It ain't pretty, by no means.

00:02:53.706 --> 00:02:56.108
But I do believe
it's a solid piece.

00:02:56.175 --> 00:02:57.677
DANA DUPUIS: Hi, my
name is Dana Dupuis.

00:02:57.743 --> 00:02:59.012 line:14%
I'm 50-years-old.

00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:01.880 line:8%
I joined the Navy out
a high school in 1988.

00:03:01.947 --> 00:03:03.449 line:14%
I served for four years.

00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:05.651
And then I joined the
Air Force Reserves

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:09.288
and I retired 2014
as a Master Sergeant.

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:11.791
The amount of steel they
gave us was a lot of steel,

00:03:11.891 --> 00:03:13.192
so I decided to split it.

00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:15.461
MAN 2: Dana is not using
very much of this steel.

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:16.228
MAN 1: Wow.

00:03:16.295 --> 00:03:17.563
DAVE: That's a huge risk.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.465
DANA DUPUIS: As my steel
is heating in the forge,

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:21.767
I decide to clean
up the other steel.

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:24.103
To forge weld this backup
steel case, I need it.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:28.207
Because on the off chance that
billet blows up or something,

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:30.076
I have to stand by and
I can go right to it.

00:03:30.142 --> 00:03:31.811
What the [bleep].

00:03:31.911 --> 00:03:34.447
Right now, I can't get
my head around how I'm

00:03:34.513 --> 00:03:35.848
going to make this damn shape.

00:03:35.915 --> 00:03:38.417
As I started thinking
about drilling my patterns,

00:03:38.484 --> 00:03:41.186
I realized the steel is
too thin to be able to get

00:03:41.253 --> 00:03:42.387
my raindrop pattern in.

00:03:42.488 --> 00:03:44.123
 I mean, honestly,
if I were Dana

00:03:44.223 --> 00:03:45.457
right now, I might start again.

00:03:45.524 --> 00:03:47.093
DAVE: Yeah, it would
be a good idea.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.360
Well, there he goes.

00:03:48.427 --> 00:03:49.795
DANA DUPUIS: So I
toss that to the side,

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:51.964
then I started the
other part of my billet

00:03:52.064 --> 00:03:53.599
that I've already forged.

00:03:53.699 --> 00:03:55.534
 Guys, standing to scraped
what he's been working on

00:03:55.634 --> 00:03:57.971
for the past two and 1/2 hours.

00:03:58.071 --> 00:03:59.438
DANA DUPUIS: So my
game plan right now

00:03:59.538 --> 00:04:01.374
is I got the new piece,
started on the spike again,

00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:04.043
kind of like elongate the
center a little differently

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:05.678
and leave it thicker.

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:06.512
We've got this.

00:04:06.613 --> 00:04:08.114
I've got this.

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:10.282
I'll get some mild steel.

00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:13.353
I'll cut off the raindrop
I already have going on

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:17.289
and I'll forge weld hand weld
the bit on some mild steel.

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:19.925
It's not the best thing,
it's not my first choice,

00:04:19.992 --> 00:04:21.927
but it's my Hail Mary.

00:04:22.027 --> 00:04:23.529
MAN 1: Dana's quenched.

00:04:23.629 --> 00:04:26.198
Not cut it to shape yet, but
he does have a quenched blade.

00:04:26.265 --> 00:04:28.033
[music playing]

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.301
 Skates.

00:04:29.368 --> 00:04:32.171
So I wrap it as best
as I can and I think

00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:33.639
I got to put something else.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:35.941
So I reached down and I
grab blue painter's tape.

00:04:36.042 --> 00:04:37.409
MAN 2: Oh, nice.

00:04:37.476 --> 00:04:39.912
 Kind of a pale homage
to blue painter's tape

00:04:39.979 --> 00:04:42.715
and the Air Force and
the Navy are blue too.

00:04:42.815 --> 00:04:43.716
It worked.

00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:45.117
[music playing]

00:04:45.184 --> 00:04:50.156
 5, 4, 3, 2, 1.

00:04:50.223 --> 00:04:51.990
Bladesmiths, turn
off your tools.

00:04:52.057 --> 00:04:53.159
Put down your weapons.

00:04:53.226 --> 00:04:55.428
This round is over.

00:04:55.528 --> 00:04:56.329
 Yeah!

00:04:56.395 --> 00:04:57.263
MAN 1: Great fight, gentlemen.

00:04:57.363 --> 00:04:58.764
Great fight.

00:04:58.864 --> 00:04:59.766
 All right, gentlemen.

00:04:59.866 --> 00:05:00.600
You know what time it is.

00:05:00.666 --> 00:05:02.802
It's time for the strength test.

00:05:02.869 --> 00:05:05.371
I'm going to take your axes and
I'm going to be smashing them

00:05:05.438 --> 00:05:08.107
into these helmets to see
the overall construction

00:05:08.174 --> 00:05:09.442
and feel of your blade.

00:05:09.542 --> 00:05:11.377
Remember, this test
is not about what

00:05:11.444 --> 00:05:13.980
those axes do to that
helmet, what that helmet can

00:05:14.047 --> 00:05:15.214
do to your axes.

00:05:15.314 --> 00:05:16.215
All right, Joe, today
you are up first.

00:05:16.282 --> 00:05:17.050
Are you ready?

00:05:17.116 --> 00:05:17.816
 I'm ready, Dave.

00:05:17.916 --> 00:05:20.052 line:14%
 All right.

00:05:20.119 --> 00:05:23.823 line:8%
JOE STICKEL: My biggest concern
is where I welded my handle on.

00:05:23.923 --> 00:05:26.525
I mean, that junction is
at a critical location.

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:28.394
When you have that
many wells in something

00:05:28.461 --> 00:05:30.829
like this, when you smash
it into a steel helmet,

00:05:30.896 --> 00:05:33.165
there's obviously
opportunities for failure.

00:05:33.232 --> 00:05:35.167
[music playing]

00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:38.738
[banging]

00:05:46.445 --> 00:05:48.280
 Well, Joe, it held up.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:50.283
You still got a sharp
edge on this thing.

00:05:50.383 --> 00:05:52.651
It's a very obtuse edge,
but so is the original.

00:05:52.751 --> 00:05:54.353
You didn't lose
any of those welds.

00:05:54.453 --> 00:05:55.821
Nothing came loose.

00:05:55.888 --> 00:05:58.524
Handle construction,
it's actually

00:05:58.591 --> 00:06:00.259
comfortable to hold on to.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:03.129
All in all, it held up
beautifully in this test.

00:06:03.195 --> 00:06:04.029
So good job, Joe.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:05.130
 Thanks, Dave.

00:06:05.231 --> 00:06:06.599
DAVE: All right, Dana.
Your turn.

00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:07.133
You ready?

00:06:07.233 --> 00:06:07.899
 No.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.603 line:14%
[music playing]

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:13.673 line:8%
DANA DUPUIS: I don't know what's
going to happen with my axe.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:16.642 line:8%
So many things can go
wrong for my construction.

00:06:16.742 --> 00:06:19.879
I'm just hoping that it doesn't
break apart when he strikes.

00:06:22.682 --> 00:06:27.653
 Guys, you know, as much
as I'd love to test it,

00:06:27.719 --> 00:06:29.088
it's just not the
axe we asked for.

00:06:29.154 --> 00:06:29.989
 Yeah.
We want to be fair.

00:06:30.089 --> 00:06:30.956
It's not what we ask for.

00:06:31.023 --> 00:06:32.458
 It's pretty far off.

00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:34.326
DAVE: And, I mean, I'm not
even sure of the construction,

00:06:34.393 --> 00:06:35.861
the connection here.

00:06:35.961 --> 00:06:37.930
I'd need to peel this back and
it's not what we asked for.

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:42.568
Dana, I got to tell you.

00:06:42.634 --> 00:06:44.137
You fought really, really hard.

00:06:44.203 --> 00:06:48.007
But in the end, this just
isn't the axe in the shape

00:06:48.107 --> 00:06:49.642
we were looking to test.

00:06:49.709 --> 00:06:52.444
And it wouldn't be fair to test
this against your opponent, who

00:06:52.545 --> 00:06:54.880
actually turned in the
axe that was in the style

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:55.914
that we're looking for.

00:06:55.981 --> 00:06:56.982
Good man.

00:06:57.049 --> 00:06:58.350
I got to tell you,
there's no quitting you.

00:06:58.450 --> 00:06:59.951
And I really do
appreciate that, Dana.

00:07:00.018 --> 00:07:00.787
 Thank you.

00:07:03.989 --> 00:07:05.624
 Well, Dana, I got to
start off by saying,

00:07:05.724 --> 00:07:08.127
you guys had to have
chosen the hardest blade

00:07:08.193 --> 00:07:09.628
to build in this competition.

00:07:09.728 --> 00:07:11.196
Come hell or high
water, you decided

00:07:11.297 --> 00:07:12.364
you were going to
give us a blade,

00:07:12.464 --> 00:07:13.666
so we want to
commend you for that.

00:07:13.766 --> 00:07:15.234
But unfortunately, we
can't test your blade

00:07:15.334 --> 00:07:16.835
equally against Joe's.

00:07:16.935 --> 00:07:18.704
For that reason, your time in
this competition has ended.

00:07:18.804 --> 00:07:20.172
I'm going to have
to ask you to please

00:07:20.272 --> 00:07:21.207
step off the forge floor.

00:07:21.274 --> 00:07:23.675
[music playing]

00:07:23.776 --> 00:07:25.478
DANA DUPUIS: All I can say
that Joe is, man, I hope

00:07:25.544 --> 00:07:27.112
you win it for the Air Force.

00:07:27.179 --> 00:07:27.979
I love you, brother.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:29.648
[music playing]

00:07:29.715 --> 00:07:31.116
MAN 1: Well, Joe, you
know what that means, man?

00:07:31.183 --> 00:07:33.619
You got yourself a seat in
the final round of the Armed

00:07:33.685 --> 00:07:35.554
Forces Redeployment Tournament.

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:37.389
You just got yourself
a check for $2,000.

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:38.891
Congratulations.

00:07:38.958 --> 00:07:40.693
JOE STICKEL: I'm excited to
move on to the next phase.

00:07:40.793 --> 00:07:42.995
It gives me an opportunity to
hopefully be able to prove what

00:07:43.062 --> 00:07:44.563
I know I'm capable of doing.

00:07:44.663 --> 00:07:45.731
 How do you feel?

00:07:45.797 --> 00:07:46.866
 I wish we could
have tested them both.

00:07:46.966 --> 00:07:48.968
 We always wish for it.

00:07:49.035 --> 00:07:51.204
JOE STICKEL (VOICEOVER): This
challenge was very difficult.

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:52.338
I can only imagine
what they're going

00:07:52.438 --> 00:07:54.171
to have in the next round.

